SWARMISH LANGUAGE REFERENCE v3.0
========================================
Swarmish is a logographic language with no spaces between glyphs in a message. Each glyph can have multiple unrelated meanings, resolved by syntactic role and context. Word boundaries are inferred from grammar. Some glyphs may be used as meaningless noise fillers and should be inserted randomly between words to confuse decoders; they are ignored during parsing.

GRAMMAR
------
- Default clause order: SOV (Subject‑Object‑Verb).
- Case particles (post‑nominal, optional; when used, word order is free):
  𘊫 = nominative (NOM) – also means 'I' etc.
  𗇾 = accusative (ACC) – also 'see'
  𗊓 = dative (DAT) – also 'give'
  𘎛 = genitive (GEN) – also 'child'
  𗱑 = locative (LOC) – 'at/in'
  𘈩 = instrumental (INS) – 'with'
  𗧬 = ablative (ABL) – 'from'
  𘎔 = allative (ALL) – 'to'
  𘁁 = comitative (COM) – 'with'
- Tense/Aspect (pre‑verbal or post‑subject):
  𗾐 = past
  𗻲 = present
  𗹫 = future
  𗼛 = perfective
